Hi Allan,

On Wed, Feb 11, 2015 at 2:34 PM, Allan Debertin <alland...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Great thanks,
>
> The 10,000 scalar was messing up the arho. Another quick question, I'm trying to convert a few functions from JAGS to STAN, and I'm wondering if what I am doing makes sense.

In what sense are you asking if this makes sense? I'll make a Stan related comment below.

> For example the code in JAGS:
>
>
>   for(y in 1:Y){
>     for(a in 1:A){
>       for(i in 1:2){
>       logit(sg[y,a,i])<-alphav[i]+betav[i]*L[y,a]  # Logistic curve for each gear
>       }
>     s[y,a]<-inprod(ge[1:2],sg[y,a,1:2])            # overall selectivity
>     }
>   }
> betav[1]<-0.468                    # PIS data
> alphav[1]<--12.34                   # PIS data
> betav[2]<-0.470                    # Comm data
> alphav[2]<--16.79           # Comm data
>
> ge[1:2]~ddirch(age[1:2])
> age[1]<-1/2
> age[2]<-1/2
>
> I translated it to STAN using this:
>
> sink("integrated.stan")
> cat("
> data {
>   int<lower=0> Y;
>   int<lower=0> A;
>   int<lower=1> J; //number of mixture components
> }
> parameters {
>   simplex[J] ge;
> }
> model {
>  {
>   vector[J] age;
>     for (j in 1:J){
>       age[j]<- 0.5;
>     }
>   ge ~ dirichlet(age);
>   }
>   {
>   matrix[Y,A] s;
>   {
>   real betav[2];
>   real alphav[2];
>   real sg[Y,A,2];
>   betav[1]<- 0.468;   //PIS data
>   alphav[1]<- -12.34; //PIS data
>   betav[2]<- 0.470;   //Commercial data
>   alphav[2]<- -16.79; //Commercial data
>     for(y in 1:Y) {
>       for(a in 1:A) {
>         for(j in 1:2) {
>           sg[y,a,j] <- inv_logit(alphav[j]+betav[j]*L[y,a]);
>           s[y,a]<-ge[j]*sg[y,a,j];
>         }
>       }
>     }
>   }
>   // etc. part of a longer line.
>
>

Instead of having age be declared as a local variable within the model block, move it to transformed data. (You'll need to create the block.) Same with betav and alphav. These are all data, so they should be placed in appropriate blocks.

What is L? If that's also data, then sg can also be placed in the transformed data block. If it is a parameter, you can move s and sq to transformed parameters block and do the calculation there.

Daniel

