Hi Bob,

I found a not-so-elegant solution to the problem above for the JAGS to STAN matrix division differences:

"2. Finally, I have a question about matrix division, particularly a matrix divided by a vector.
So in JAGS this statement works and produces numbers:
pc[y,a] <-g[y,a] /delta[y] //delta[y] is sum(g[y,a]
"

I'm wondering if there is a better way then displayed below:

So lets pretend I have a matrix with 5 rows, and 3 columns and a matrix of 5 rows, and 1 column . I want to divide each row

This is R Code of what I want to do more elegantly in STAN:
X <- matrix(c(rep(1:5,3)),nrow=5)
X

y<- matrix(c(5:1), nrow=5)
y

X / y #non-conformable

a<-X[1,1:3] / y[1,1]
b<-X[2,1:3] / y[2,1]
c<-X[3,1:3] / y[3,1]
d<-X[4,1:3] / y[4,1]
e<-X[5,1:3] / y[5,1]

result<- matrix(c(a,b,c,d,e),byrow=T,nrow=5)
> result
     [,1] [,2] [,3]
[1,]  0.2  0.2  0.2
[2,]  0.5  0.5  0.5
[3,]  1.0  1.0  1.0
[4,]  2.0  2.0  2.0
[5,]  5.0  5.0  5.0

So to implement in STAN this is what I did:

matrix[I,J] X;  //5 rows by 3 column
matrix[I,1] y;  //5 rows by 1 column
row_vector[I,J] Z //result product as above

    for (i in 1:I) {
      for(j in 1:J) {
          y[i,1] <- sum(X[i]);
          z[1,j] <- X[1,j] / y[1,1];
          z[2,j] <- X[2,j] / y[2,1];
          z[3,j] <- X[3,j] / y[3,1];
          z[4,j] <- X[4,j] / y[4,1];
          z[5,j] <- X[5,j] / y[5,1];
      }
    }

There's got to be a better way of doing this.

