> In C++ code, this pstream__ points to an output stream (typically the standard ouptput stream).  So in rstan, it would be just &Rcpp::Rcout. A function without argument pstream__ can be created to be called in R and this function calls the function with the additional parameter pstream__.

Makes sense. At the moment, I've got these lines at the end of the function that exposes the user-defined functions to R

  # try to compile
  compiled <- sourceCpp(code = paste(lines, collapse = "\n"))
  sapply(compiled$functions, FUN = function(x) {
    fun <- get(x, envir = .GlobalEnv)
    formals(fun)$pstream__ <- new('externalptr')
    assign(x, fun, envir = .GlobalEnv)
  })
  return(invisible(NULL))

So, is there something better to make fun's default to the pstream__ than a new('externalptr')? At this point, can I somehow point to &Rcpp::Rcout so that user-defined functions could print? Or do I need to add more logic to the C++ parsing so that the default argument to pstream__ is &Rcpp::Rcout, as in

// [[Rcpp::export]]
matrix_d
normalize_m(const matrix_d& z, std::ostream* pstream__) {

would become

// [[Rcpp::export]]
matrix_d
normalize_m(const matrix_d& z, std::ostream* pstream__ = &Rcpp::Rcout) {

and then try to not pass anything from R for pstream__ to override that default.

Thanks,
Ben

