Jonah,
Thank you very much for your answer. A few points:

1. About declaring l and b in transformed parameters. In my previous exponential smoothing type models, this worked well - no compiler warnings and the fit was OK. Also, I see this example in the Stan manual (p67)

transformed parameters {

real<lower=0> sigma[T];

sigma[1] <- sigma1;    //<--------- and sigma1 is declared in data section

for (t in 2:T)

sigma[t] <- sqrt(alpha0

+ alpha1 * pow(r[t-1] - mu, 2)

+ beta1 * pow(sigma[t-1], 2));

}

2. Yes, trendError and levelError should be just parameters - thank you!
3. Correct, y is positive, while error can go both mays, so occasionally I get rejections, but this is not a major problem.
4. I put a minimum value on sigma, because in my previous experience with fitting the Student distributions, when I allowed nu to go to 1 and sigma to 0, I was getting numerical problems. Is it really important to have the same bound in declaration and model? Because the model statement is more restrictive.

Thank you again. I think I can proceed now.

Slawek

On Tue, Feb 17, 2015 at 4:27 PM, Jonah <jga...@gmail.com> wrote:

> I don't know enough about these models to speak to the statistics behind it, but a few things seem a bit strange :
>
> - You've declared your data y to be positive but you're using the student_t distribution which has support on the negative reals too.
> - Why does sigma have to be greater than 0.05? Unless that's some peculiarity about these models, you're probably better of without the truncation. Maybe try a prior that doesn't have a lot of mass right near 0 but don't use the hard constraint unless you have to for some reason. Furthermore, MIN_SIGMA doesn't have the same value as the lower bound on sigma when it's declared as a parameter. If you absolutely need to truncate sigma slightly above zero then you should declare that value as the lower bound in the parameters block rather than 0.
>
> Hopefully somebody else who is more familiar with these models will have some other and better suggestions.
>
> On Tuesday, February 17, 2015 at 6:51:42 PM UTC-5, Jonah wrote:
