> Bob,
>
> Jumpy means that trace looks like piece-wise linear.  It looks like there is no mixing.  I thought maybe this is somewhat related to step size.

Sometimes reducing initial step size and increasing
target acceptance rate can help.

> I am using RStan.  Can you suggest what argument I should use for pairs?

fit <- stan(...);
pairs(fit);

You need to be sure to use more than one chain, as it plots
half the chains above diagonal and half below.

Often when there are identifiability problems, two
parameters will be highly correlated.

> Anyway, I have tried negative binomial.  It looks better and Rhat of lp__ is 1.00 while Rhat for parameter beta is 1.16.  I am still not comfortable with the mixing for beta.  Trace is attached as well as code.

Those traceplots don't look so bad.  (You can drop the
warmup iterations using

  inc_warmup=FALSE

Does beta look better if you run for more iterations?
And I'd suggest making both warmup and sampling longer.

> Since model is not identifiable you have suggested some time ago to set B[1] to 0 and identify parameters A and B via priors.  Is there a better method?

Not that I know of.

I'd still suggest simulating data and see if you
can fit the model to verify that it's not just
an issue with your data.  In fact, I'd recommend that
for any model.

- Bob

